Nancy Lebovitz has had some disagreements with me in the past, I am
sure, but she has maintained a polite tone for the most part even in
her disagreements or even frustration. I welcome this tone, and hence
will take some time to repond to her points (this was the same reason
I spent time responding to Blanc Weber's questions about PPL).

> I was wondering how what you had to say about the urban poor as
> "human garbage" fitted with what you said about the destructiveness
> of various government programs. (I'm paraphrasing from memory.) 

Rightly or wrongly, in terms of who caused what, the urban poor are
"Preterite," that is, they have been passed-over and cannot at this
point be saved. A small percentage can, of course, but the bulk of
them are simply too far gone...no system I can imagine can convert
them into "little cogs in the machine," to use Fabijanic's disdainful
term for doctors, engineers, teachers, writers, and other such folks.

I don't claim the inner city folks _have_ to or _should_ become
productive contributors and the like, I just claim that the rest of us
owe them nothing, have little to trade with them (we being cogs who
want something of value to us in transactions....except they have
nothing to offer us, for the most part).

Being Preterite, they are effectively already on the ash heap of
history. Sorry to sound so blunt, but it's true, and Extropians should
not shrink from the truth the way so many do-gooders (who actually do
bad) insist on doing.

> Another factor seems to be the design of mass transit systems to
> serve surbanites who work in the city. I'd even be willing to bet
> that some of the sales tax that the urban working poor pay goes
> to support those systems.

Much worse than "mass trasit," generally, was the huge
highway-building binge of the 1950s. It emptied out the inner cities
of educated workers, who moved out to the suburbs which were now
livable and commutable, and created much of the problem we are talking
about. 

It also distorted the economics of railroads, due to the subsidies for
cars--remember that the Calfornia "freeways" are just that. 

> There was a recent WSJ article about revising bus schedules to
> make it easier for poor people to work outside the city--it's
> a lot cheaper than job programs. However, my guess is that the
> idea is too respectful of poor people to catch on and doesn't
> supply enough government jobs for people who want to help for
> a living, and letting poor people make money by selling rides in 
> jitneys is unthinkable....

Precisely! In fact, letting poor people operate their won taxis and
jitneys would do wonders for several problems, which I don't need to
go into here.

> I've heard that one of the factors in all this was the rise of
> the white unions--they kept black people out of the building trades.

That's right. And the government sanction of the unions meant blacks
were screwed. (In South Africa, at the end of the Second World War,
American and RSA companies wanted to hire blacks to help meet the
labor needs....that's when RSA instituted Apartheid.)

> This matter of responsibility is an interesting question. People
> in Haiti aren't being personally coerced by the sanctions for the
> most part, but I think that they could put a lot of blame on
> the US government for their situation. 

Agreed, and all libertarians I know are against such sanctions, no
matter how much we may dislike the dictator in charge...should Haiti
put sanctions in place on us just because they don't like King Bill?

> Would a poor person in the US who has had their choices in housing
