I'd say she is being coerced. The trade import agreement on Japanese
cars in the early 1980s had the effect of making _all_ cars more
expensive. To an inner city mother who can't afford a car, and hence
can't get to the suburbs where the jobs are, thats' coercion.

> If the licensing and credentialling that prevent a lot of jobs
> are ended at the same time, it might actually work. 

That's right. The natural entrepreneurial spirit of even these
"Preterites" might even be enough to save many of them. And withought
licensing or zoning, etc., we could have maid service and the like
(don't laugh, I'mn not being facetious or "outrageous." I'm quite
serious....why not give some of my money to someone who's willing to
watch my kids, clean my house, etc.? Nothing ignoble about that,
except when the Government interferes.)

